{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-152279",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x+2 \\\\ y=2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+2 & =2-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ x(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=0.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2-2{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2-2(-4) \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,10).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2-2{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2-2(0) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=10,\\,{y}_{2}=2,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=10+2=12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227274/assets/941d698059cfd145f263cbdde048fc4a32245cdad4bc6bb6d7a504d005b6c231.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
